    "actClause": "AN ACT to amend the domestic relations law, in relation to modifying the\nprovisions regarding notice of a matrimonial action where the complaint is not\npersonally served with the summons",
    "basePrintNo": "S9784",
    "basePrintNoStr": "S9784-2023",
    "coSponsors": {
     "items": [],
     "size": 0
    },
    "fullText": "",
    "fullTextFormats": [],
    "fullTextHtml": "",
    "fullTextTemplate": "",
    "lawCode": "Amd §232, Dom Rel L",
    "lawSection": "Domestic Relations Law",
    "memo": " \nBILL NUMBER: S9784\n\nSPONSOR: HOYLMAN-SIGAL\n \nTITLE OF BILL:\n\nAn act to amend the domestic relations law, in relation to modifying the\nprovisions regarding notice of a matrimonial action where the complaint\nis not personally served with the summons\n\n2.  \nSOURCE OF BILL\n\nThis bill is being introduced at the request of the Judiciary, on the\nrecommendation of the Chief Administrative Judge's Matrimonial Practice\nAdvisory and Rules Committee.\n\n3.  \nPURPOSE OF BILL\n\nThis measure would modify the provisions regarding how notice of a\nmatrimonial action must be given in order to permit the court to take a\ndefault judgment when the complaint is not personally served with the\nsummons and service is \"impracticable\" under paragraphs (1), ( 2), and\n(4) of CPLR § 308, by permitting court-ordered alternative service of\nthe divorce summons by email in addition to publication pursuant to CPLR\n§ 315.\n\n4. \nSUMMARY OF PROVISIONS\n\nSection 1 of the bill amends Domestic Relations Law (DRL) § 232(a) to\nadd to the circumstances under which a default judgment may be entered\nupon defendant's default in appearing or pleading, the fact that service\nof the summons or summons and complaint was made by email through proof\nof an active email account of the defendant which is shown to be reason-\nably calculated to give notice to the defendant.  As used in this\nsection, an active email account is one that is shown to have been used\nby the defendant in the last thirty days.\n\nSection 1 also amends DRL § 232(b) to replace statutory references to\n\"he\" with \"such person\" and \"affiant or officer, who executed the affi-\ndavit or certificate\", respectively.  1\n\nSection 2 of the bill fixes an immediate effective date for the bill.\n\n5.  \nJUSTIFICATION\n\nThis bill will further access to justice by increasing the likelihood\nthat defendants will receive notice of a divorce. Under DRL § 232(a) as\ncurrently written, when the defendant cannot be found for service and\nfails to answer, the only method of service which would permit the court\nto enter a default judgment is court-ordered service pursuant to CPLR §\n308 (which cannot be accomplished if defendant cannot be found and has\nleft no address) or court-ordered service by publication as a last\nresort (which is expensive, time consuming, unlikely to reach the\ndefendant, and increasingly difficult with the dwindling number of print\npublications).\n\nThis bill will increase the likelihood that defendants receive notice of\nthe divorce and are able to protect their important interests in the\ndivorce as to custody of their children and distribution of their prop-\nerty, and avoid default. Plaintiffs will also benefit not only from the\nreduced expenses of service by email rather than publication, but also\nfrom being able to work out the details of the divorce with defendants\nbefore the Judgment of Divorce is signed, thereby avoiding expensive and\nlengthy post-judgment litigation brought by defendants who did not\nrespond to the summons. The court system will also benefit from the\nreduction in post-judgment matrimonial litigation.\n\nWhile personal service upon a defendant is required in a divorce action,\nCPLR § 308 allows several other methods if delivery to the defendant is\nnot possible. These include service on a person of suitable age and\ndiscretion with simultaneous mailing (CPLR § 308(2)); service on an\nagent designated pursuant to Rule 318 for service (CPLR § 308(3)): and\n\"nail and mail\" service where service pursuant to subdivisions one and\ntwo cannot be made with due diligence (CPLR § 308(4).\n\nAll three subdivisions provide an exception in matrimonial actions where\nservice may be made pursuant to a court order in accordance with DRL §\n232(a). CPLR § 308(5) provides for service \"in such manner as the court,\nupon motion without notice, directs, if service is impracticable under\nparagraphs one, two and four of this section.\"  DRL § 232 requires that\nif the complaint is not personally served with the summons, no default\njudgment may be granted unless the summons gives notice of the nature of\nthe divorce action on its face and has either been personally delivered\nto the defendant or served on defendant in accordance with either CPLR\n308 or CPLR 315. CPLR 315 permits service by publication by court order\non motion without notice as a last resort \"if service cannot be made by\nanother prescribed method with due diligence\" in certain types of\nactions, one of which is a matrimonial action.\n\nThese provisions have caused problems in divorce actions where the\nplaintiff cannot locate the defendant, because the defendant has left no\nforwarding address or contact information. Supreme Courts frequently\nfeel compelled to order publication as a method of last resort. As stat-\ned by the Court of Appeals in upholding an order of the Third Department\nordering service by publication to be paid by the county for an indigent\nlitigant: \"We are not unmindful, however, of the cost of publication in\na matrimonial action and the burden it may impose on local government.\nOur affirmance is, therefore, without prejudice to the parties, if so\nadvised, to apply for a determination whether, in a matrimonial action,\njudicially devised service (CPLR 308, subd. 5) is available as an alter-\nnative to service by publication.\" Deason v. Deason, 32 N.Y.2d 93, 95,\n296 N.E.2d 229, 230 (1973). Thus, the Court of Appeals encouraged courts\nto devise means of alternative service pursuant to CPLR 308(5) whenever\npossible.\n\nBack in 1973 when Deason was decided, email was not available to use as\na method of alternative service. Today, the situation is totally differ-\nent as email is one of the most frequently used means of communication.\nNevertheless, although there have been cases where courts have fashioned\nalternative service in matrimonial cases pursuant to CPLR 308(5), use of\nemail is rarely used in Supreme Court matrimonial actions. In a 1992\ncase, the court refused to grant service by publication and instead\nordered service by a combination of mailing to defendant's mother, and\nposting in several places, stating:\n\n\"The court recognizes that where constructive means of service are\nsought under CPLR 308 (5), publication is traditionally requested and\nroutinely granted either solely or in conjunction with some other deliv-\nery means. This habit may spring from other statutes that specify publi-\ncation as a means of service. But a literal reading of CPLR 308 (5) and\nthe cases construing it and its predecessors lead this court to conclude\nthat publication under subdivision (5) is not an essential or necessary\nelement nor even a desirable element of judicially devised service.\"\n\nSee Debra M v. Guy M, 155 Misc. 2d 912, 914, 591 N.Y.S.2d 302 (Sup.  Ct.\n1992).\n\nThis addresses this problem by amending DRL § 232(a) to give official\nlegislative recognition to use of email as a legitimate method of court\nordered alternative service pursuant to CPLR 308(5) in matrimonial\nactions in accordance with the encouragement of the Court of Appeals in\nDeason v. Deason, supra.\n\nThis proposal will ensure that service by publication in matrimonial\ncases, will be used as a last resort only, and will encourage courts to\norder alternative service by email where the court is satisfied that\nservice under paragraphs 1, 2 and 4 of CPLR § 308 is impracticable, and\nthat there is proof that the email account is active, and that the plat-\nform to be used is reasonably calculated to reach the defendant. The\nterm \"active\" in the proposal is defined to mean that it has been used\nwithin the last thirty days in order to prevent litigation.\n\nThe bill satisfies the demands of due process, which requires that\ndefendant be given notice of the divorce action by a method reasonably\ncalculated to reach the defendant, but does not require that the defend-\nant receive actual notice of the divorce action where the defendant\ncannot be located. As stated in Hollow v. Hollow, service in a divorce\ncase must be sufficient to satisfy the demands of due process under the\nstatute permitting alternative methods for service of process. (See\nHollow v. Hollow, 193 Misc. 2d 691, 694, 747 N.Y.S.2d 704, 707 (Sup. Ct.\n2002). However, it need not always provide actual notice to the defend-\nant as stated by the Court of Appeals in Dobkin v. Chapman in upholding\nmethods of alternative service ordered pursuant to CPLR 308 in three\nautomobile accident cases resulting in injury or death where defendants\ncould not be found and normal methods of service were impossible: \"It is\nquite clear that the draftsmen of section 308 deemed it not at all\nunlikely that the method of service directed under its paragraph 4 would\noccasionally result in failure to bring actual notice to a defendant.\"\n(Dobkin v. Chapman, 21 N.Y.2d 490, 500, 236 N.E.2d 451, 456 (1968).\nFurther, the Court of Appeals stated: \"Due process is not, however, a\nmechanical formula or a rigid set of rules. Increasingly in modern\njurisprudence, the term has come to represent a realistic and reasonable\nevaluation of the respective interests of plaintiffs, defendants and the\nstate under the circumstances of the particular case.\" Id. at 457-58).\n\n6.  \nPRIOR LEGISLATIVE HISTORY\n\nA similar bill was submitted by the Judiciary in the 2019-20 legislative\nsession (OCA-89) and the 2021-22 legislative session\n\n(OCA-58).\n\n7.  \nFISCAL IMPLICATIONS\n\nThis bill would have no meaningful State fiscal impact.\n\n8.  \nEFFECTIVE DATE\nThis bill would take effect immediately.\n",
